{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: please review the adversarial evidence failure modes at exact clean head 31ddeb38f8449f88bb9f8d2422572045a527fb20 on request/spike-evidence, artifact f3e0610627f951fee380881b6e7e12274d3c727d, governed by request dcc02574782bd313e4187c16496c63ef98cfcea3.\n\nA red make spike printed one word for two events that ask different things of a reader: a named security property failing, and the harness dying before it could tell us. A run now reports pass, fail, or error, and a real failure outranks a dead runner when both occur so the security finding is never softened into a runner complaint. Both still exit non-zero. .github/workflows/ci.yml uploads the run evidence on failure with a thirty-day retention.\n\nThree things I would most like challenged.\n\nThe severity ordering is a judgement, not a derivation. I made a skipped or never-reported test an error rather than a fail, on the reasoning that it leaves the property unestablished rather than shows it broken. Someone could reasonably argue a named adversarial test that vanishes should be as loud as one that fails, since a test silently dropped from a package is a way to lose coverage without anyone noticing. If you think that, say so and I will make absence a fail.\n\nThe diagnosis condition I did not satisfy by fixing. The intermittent's cause is named — before c9dc187d the runner executed the whole repository suite, so any unrelated flaky test set runErr and printed fail while all six cases passed — and c9dc187d already fixed it, two hours after the request was filed. My evidence is five of five failing CI runs being this step with no test output, every run since being green, and ten consecutive green local runs. I cannot say which test failed in those specific runs because the evidence was discarded, which is the defect the head closes. Please judge whether naming an already-fixed cause satisfies a condition that asked for diagnosis, or whether that should have been a separate finding